1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:02,220
Two feet wide. Zero visibility.

2
00:00:02,220 --> 00:00:10,521
An American rescueman feels his way through a flooded cave passage, passing a sedated child hand to hand, in the pitch dark.

3
00:00:11,666 --> 00:00:15,219
His name has never been in a single movie made about this rescue.

4
00:00:16,366 --> 00:00:20,367
Neither has the second Thai Navy diver who died because of this mission.

5
00:00:20,367 --> 00:00:23,445
Or the federal jury trial a billionaire's tweet actually produced.

6
00:00:24,566 --> 00:00:30,287
This is the real story of the Tham Luang cave rescue, not the one you already watched in a theater or on Netflix.

7
00:00:31,433 --> 00:00:35,151
Twelve boys and their coach went into a cave in northern Thailand.

8
00:00:35,151 --> 00:00:36,700
All thirteen came out alive.

9
00:00:36,700 --> 00:00:40,419
Almost nothing else about that sentence is as simple as it sounds.

10
00:00:41,566 --> 00:00:43,133
You're watching mindwired.

11
00:00:44,266 --> 00:01:01,350
This is the full, real story of the 2018 Tham Luang cave rescue: what actually happened inside the cave, the American rescue team every film left out, the federal trial a billionaire's tweet produced, and the real 2026 echo that shows this story isn't finished yet.

12
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:04,616
Chapter one. The cave.

13
00:01:05,733 --> 00:01:08,743
June twenty-third, twenty eighteen. Chiang Rai, northern Thailand.

14
00:01:08,743 --> 00:01:17,775
Twelve boys from the Wild Boars youth football club, ages eleven to sixteen, walk into Tham Luang cave with their assistant coach after practice.

15
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:20,455
Coach Ekkapol Chantawong is twenty-five.

16
00:01:20,455 --> 00:01:29,166
He was orphaned as a child, spent years as a novice Buddhist monk, and is legally stateless, along with at least three of the boys on this team.

17
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:37,120
Unseasonal monsoon rain floods the cave's lower passages while they're inside, cutting off the exit and pushing the group deeper.

18
00:01:37,120 --> 00:01:42,577
That evening, the head coach, a different man named Nopparat, finds twenty missed calls from parents.

19
00:01:43,700 --> 00:01:48,959
Thai Navy Seal divers reach a chamber called Pattaya Beach two days later, and turn back.

20
00:01:48,959 --> 00:01:51,589
The water beyond it has never been mapped.

21
00:01:52,733 --> 00:01:54,731
Cave diving is not scuba diving.

22
00:01:54,731 --> 00:02:06,055
In zero visibility, in a current, a diver has to feel the walls with both hands, laying a guide line as they go, because turning around and simply swimming back is not always possible.

23
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:18,668
That is why this call went out not to the Thai military alone, but to a small, specific community of civilian cave-diving experts scattered across Britain, Australia, Belgium, and beyond.

24
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:20,436
June twenty-ninth.

25
00:02:20,436 --> 00:02:29,988
Thailand's Prime Minister visits the site in person, and asks publicly that the country stay focused on the rescue, not on blaming a coach for taking children into a cave.

26
00:02:31,133 --> 00:02:34,842
Chapter two. Thirteen days, and a country's worth of water.

27
00:02:35,966 --> 00:02:45,840
Within a week, the operation scales past six hundred people, then past ten thousand: engineers, soldiers, and volunteers from seventeen countries.

28
00:02:46,966 --> 00:02:55,593
Over a hundred farmers watch roughly fifty-five hundred acres of their own rice fields deliberately flooded, diverted on purpose to slow water pouring into the mountain.

29
00:02:55,593 --> 00:02:59,243
It is barely mentioned in any film made about this rescue.

30
00:03:00,366 --> 00:03:11,755
More than one billion litres of water are pumped out of the mountain, roughly four hundred Olympic swimming pools, while engineers drill over a hundred boreholes from the surface hunting for a shaft that never finds them.

31
00:03:12,900 --> 00:03:16,155
Officials debate teaching the boys to dive out themselves.

32
00:03:16,155 --> 00:03:22,304
Rejected: they are untrained children in a technical cave system that has already turned back Navy SEALs.

33
00:03:23,433 --> 00:03:29,663
Waiting out the roughly four-month monsoon season with food ferried in was the safest plan on paper.

34
00:03:29,663 --> 00:03:36,259
It was rejected too: dwindling oxygen, rising water, and thirteen people who could not simply wait that long.

35
00:03:37,400 --> 00:03:43,343
By July fourth, pumps are removing water at a peak rate of one point six million litres an hour.

36
00:03:43,343 --> 00:03:50,226
Engineers will end up drilling past a hundred boreholes into the mountain, searching for a shaft that never once finds the group.

37
00:03:51,366 --> 00:03:54,213
Chapter three. The Americans nobody put in the movie.

38
00:03:55,333 --> 00:03:59,629
June twenty-eighth. A team from the 353rd Special Operations Group, U.S.

39
00:03:59,629 --> 00:04:03,144
Air Force, lands in Chiang Rai from Okinawa, Japan.

40
00:04:04,266 --> 00:04:16,622
Two of their pararescuemen, Tech Sergeants Jamie Brisbin and John Merchand, are stationed inside a two-foot-wide, zero-visibility flooded passage called Sump Three, moving the boys hand to hand between chambers.

41
00:04:17,766 --> 00:04:21,319
"Neither of the two big films about this rescue mentions them by name."

42
00:04:22,466 --> 00:04:26,572
This is not the same as saying the divers you've heard of didn't matter.

43
00:04:26,572 --> 00:04:31,557
It means the full team was bigger, and more international, than either screen version had room for.

44
00:04:32,700 --> 00:04:49,706
The technique itself, moving a person hand-to-hand through a passage too narrow to swim through side by side, was later demonstrated using a training dummy at a US base in Japan, for exactly this reason: the real thing happened in total darkness, with no camera able to survive the trip.

45
00:04:50,833 --> 00:04:56,758
British divers Jason Mallinson and Chris Jewell joined the extraction team as well, alongside Volanthen and Stanton.

46
00:04:56,758 --> 00:05:03,032
By the final count, thirteen international cave divers and five Thai Navy SEALs made up the extraction team.

47
00:05:04,166 --> 00:05:05,968
Chapter four. Found.

48
00:05:07,100 --> 00:05:09,237
July second, evening. Nine days in.

49
00:05:09,237 --> 00:05:19,926
British divers John Volanthen and Rick Stanton, laying line two and a half kilometers into the flooded system, surface at a mud slope and find all thirteen of them alive.

50
00:05:21,066 --> 00:05:26,552
The exchange, caught on tape: "How many of you?" "Thirteen?" "Thirteen! Brilliant."

51
00:05:27,699 --> 00:05:32,157
Nine days with almost no food. All thirteen conscious, coherent, and alive.

52
00:05:32,157 --> 00:05:39,585
It is, to this day, one of the only rescues of its kind in recorded history to end this way.

53
00:05:40,733 --> 00:05:48,545
Two freelance divers, Ben Reymenants and Maksym Polejaka, reportedly reached the group first, ahead of the British team every retelling credits.

54
00:05:48,545 --> 00:05:53,010
It would take three years before Volanthen and Stanton acknowledged them publicly.

55
00:05:54,133 --> 00:06:01,190
Australian anaesthetist Richard Harris and his diving partner, veterinarian Craig Challen, are flown in specifically for what comes next.

56
00:06:01,190 --> 00:06:06,019
Harris will end up making the single riskiest call of the entire operation.

57
00:06:07,166 --> 00:06:09,674
Chapter five. The billionaire's submarine.

58
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:11,442
July eighth.

59
00:06:11,442 --> 00:06:18,192
Elon Musk tweets that a child-sized submersible, built from a spare Falcon 9 rocket part, is on its way to help.

60
00:06:19,333 --> 00:06:23,348
Two days later, the rescue is completed without it.

61
00:06:23,348 --> 00:06:30,487
The operation's on-site commander, Narongsak Osatanakorn, calls the device impractical for the cave's narrow, twisting passages.

62
00:06:31,633 --> 00:06:42,474
The device itself, nicknamed "Wild Boar" after the trapped team, was built by SpaceX and Boring Company engineers out of a spare Falcon 9 rocket part, and actually flown to the site.

63
00:06:43,600 --> 00:06:57,001
On July thirteenth, British caver Vernon Unsworth, who had lived locally and helped coordinate the rescue, tells CNN the submarine was a "PR stunt," and that Musk should, in his words, "stick his submarine where it hurts."

64
00:06:58,133 --> 00:07:07,041
Two days after that, Musk fires back on Twitter: "Sorry pedo guy, you really did ask for it." He deletes it and apologizes within days.

65
00:07:08,166 --> 00:07:11,092
Chapter six. The trial nobody filmed.

66
00:07:12,233 --> 00:07:17,183
Unsworth sues for defamation that September, seeking one hundred ninety million dollars.

67
00:07:17,183 --> 00:07:24,197
In private emails to a reporter, Musk goes further, calling Unsworth a child rapist, with no evidence.

68
00:07:25,333 --> 00:07:36,487
Musk goes further still, in a private email exchange with a reporter, calling Unsworth a "child rapist" and claiming, with no evidence at all, that he had moved to Thailand for a child bride.

69
00:07:37,633 --> 00:07:41,100
The reporter had not agreed to keep any of it off the record.

70
00:07:41,100 --> 00:07:42,701
He published the emails in full.

71
00:07:43,833 --> 00:07:53,080
A judge rules Unsworth a private citizen, not a public figure, for these statements, meaning Musk would only need to have acted carelessly, not maliciously, to lose.

72
00:07:54,200 --> 00:08:02,727
That single ruling matters more than it sounds: a public figure has to prove a defendant knew a statement was false, or recklessly ignored the truth.

73
00:08:02,727 --> 00:08:05,679
A private citizen only has to prove ordinary carelessness.

74
00:08:05,679 --> 00:08:08,959
The bar for Unsworth to win had just gotten lower.

75
00:08:10,100 --> 00:08:14,712
December third, twenty nineteen. A four-day jury trial in Los Angeles. Musk testifies.

76
00:08:14,712 --> 00:08:21,098
He apologizes to Unsworth on the stand and says no one actually understood the tweet to mean pedophile.

77
00:08:22,233 --> 00:08:36,444
Musk's defense, laid out at trial: that "pedo guy" was old South African slang for a creepy old man, not a literal accusation, and that the tweet, which never actually named Unsworth, couldn't reasonably be read as being about him specifically.

78
00:08:37,566 --> 00:08:45,507
Unsworth's team sought one hundred ninety million dollars in damages, pointing to Musk's roughly twenty billion dollar net worth at the time.

79
00:08:45,507 --> 00:08:53,448
The judge instructed the jury the private emails could describe Musk's state of mind, but could not, on their own, prove defamation.

80
00:08:54,566 --> 00:08:59,878
The jury deliberates for under an hour. They find Musk not liable. Zero damages.

81
00:08:59,878 --> 00:09:02,533
A civil verdict, not a criminal one.

82
00:09:03,666 --> 00:09:09,553
By one juror's own account afterward, the panel never even reached the question of whether the accusation was true.

83
00:09:09,553 --> 00:09:14,820
They simply weren't convinced a reasonable person could tell an un-named tweet was about Unsworth at all.

84
00:09:15,966 --> 00:09:19,675
Chapter seven. The extraction nobody could have filmed.

85
00:09:20,800 --> 00:09:25,207
Oxygen inside the boys' chamber falls toward fifteen percent, well below breathable.

86
00:09:25,207 --> 00:09:32,921
Officials abandon every plan except one: sedate all thirteen, and carry them out through the flooded cave, one at a time.

87
00:09:34,066 --> 00:09:36,811
Every other option had already failed or been rejected.

88
00:09:36,811 --> 00:09:44,437
This was the last plan left on the table, and nobody involved was certain it would work on a single child, let alone all thirteen.

89
00:09:45,566 --> 00:09:58,888
The real drug protocol, published later in the New England Journal of Medicine by the operation's own medical team: an anti-anxiety tablet, atropine, then a ketamine injection, chosen specifically because it doesn't suppress breathing.

90
00:10:00,033 --> 00:10:11,919
Ketamine specifically, because unlike almost every other sedative available, it doesn't suppress the drive to keep breathing, something that mattered enormously for a swim that would last hours through cold water.

91
00:10:13,066 --> 00:10:20,302
Each boy is fitted with a full-face oxygen mask and physically carried through the flooded passages by a lead diver, sedated the entire way.

92
00:10:21,433 --> 00:10:24,637
The order they came out in wasn't decided by the doctors.

93
00:10:24,637 --> 00:10:33,084
The boys decided it themselves, days earlier, trapped in the dark: whoever lived farthest from the cave should go first, so they wouldn't have as far to bike home.

94
00:10:34,233 --> 00:10:34,956
July sixth.

95
00:10:34,956 --> 00:10:41,822
Thai Navy Seal volunteer Saman Kunan runs out of his own air returning from delivering oxygen tanks, and dies.

96
00:10:41,822 --> 00:10:45,074
He is posthumously promoted seven ranks by royal command.

97
00:10:46,200 --> 00:10:53,396
Eighteen months later, a second Seal, Petty Officer Beirut Pakbara, dies from a blood infection contracted during the same mission.

98
00:10:53,396 --> 00:10:57,354
Almost no retelling of this story mentions his name at all.

99
00:10:58,500 --> 00:11:03,707
Two deaths, on two completely different timelines, both caused by the same eighteen-day mission.

100
00:11:03,707 --> 00:11:06,311
Most retellings only remember one of them.

101
00:11:07,433 --> 00:11:08,122
July tenth.

102
00:11:08,122 --> 00:11:18,122
Eighteen divers, thirteen from around the world and five Thai Navy SEALs, bring out the last four boys, their coach, and the medical team who never left their side.

103
00:11:18,122 --> 00:11:21,226
Eighteen days after they walked in, everyone is out.

104
00:11:22,366 --> 00:11:29,489
By the third and final day, what had taken roughly three hours per boy on day one had been cut to just over two.

105
00:11:29,489 --> 00:11:33,050
The team had gotten faster with every single life they carried out.

106
00:11:34,200 --> 00:11:36,891
Chapter eight. What the story left out.

107
00:11:38,033 --> 00:11:42,509
Freelance divers Ben Reymenants and Maksym Polejaka reportedly located the group first.

108
00:11:42,509 --> 00:11:47,359
The British divers every film credits didn't publicly acknowledge them for three years.

109
00:11:48,500 --> 00:11:50,521
Not because their contribution was invented.

110
00:11:50,521 --> 00:11:58,609
Because the two men every screen adaptation lionizes hadn't been the ones to find them first, and that fact took years to become public.

111
00:11:59,733 --> 00:12:12,402
Within weeks, Thailand fast-tracked citizenship for Ekkapol and the stateless boys on the team, members of the Tai Lue ethnic minority who had lived in Thailand their whole lives without ever legally belonging to it.

112
00:12:13,533 --> 00:12:27,090
Ekkapol and the boys had lived their entire lives in Thailand, gone to Thai schools, spoken Thai, without ever holding the legal right to a Thai passport, a bank account, or in some cases, even to travel outside their home district.

113
00:12:28,233 --> 00:12:34,450
Within days of the rescue, studios and streamers were racing to buy the rights to tell these boys' stories.

114
00:12:34,450 --> 00:12:40,667
Netflix's exclusive deal is the specific reason the Oscar-nominated documentary team never got to speak with them at all.

115
00:12:41,800 --> 00:12:54,731
That auction is why the boys never appear at all in the Oscar-nominated documentary about their own rescue, and why the scripted Netflix series exists specifically because it, not the documentary team, held the rights to tell their side of it.

116
00:12:55,866 --> 00:13:07,867
The documentary that did get real access, built from eighty-seven hours of footage the Thai Navy eventually handed over after two years of negotiation, went on to win a Bafta nomination and a string of Critics' Choice awards.

117
00:13:07,867 --> 00:13:10,077
It was still snubbed at the Oscars.

118
00:13:11,200 --> 00:13:14,047
Chapter nine. Still diving, eight years later.

119
00:13:15,166 --> 00:13:16,921
May twenty-nineteenth, twenty twenty-six.

120
00:13:16,921 --> 00:13:22,623
Eight villagers panning for gold enter a flooded mine in Xaysomboun Province, Laos.

121
00:13:22,623 --> 00:13:25,693
Monsoon rain traps seven of them underground.

122
00:13:26,833 --> 00:13:35,819
Roughly a hundred rescuers respond, specialists from Thailand, Finland, France, Indonesia, Malaysia, Japan, and Australia.

123
00:13:36,966 --> 00:13:44,280
Two of the divers who answer the call are Mikko Paasi and Norraseth Palasri, the same men who dove into Tham Luang in 2018.

124
00:13:45,400 --> 00:13:50,729
This time, it isn't a clean ending. Five survivors are found alive by May thirtieth.

125
00:13:50,729 --> 00:13:52,505
Two miners are never located.

126
00:13:52,505 --> 00:13:59,611
A cave-in and a real earthquake make the site too unstable, and on June sixth, officials call off the search.

127
00:14:00,733 --> 00:14:04,611
One miner is pulled out in a high-risk underwater evacuation on May twenty-ninth.

128
00:14:04,611 --> 00:14:09,980
The last four walk out on their own the next day, once the water finally drops low enough.

129
00:14:11,100 --> 00:14:22,019
A few weeks later, Chiang Rai holds its eighth anniversary memorial for the Tham Luang rescue: a tribute to Saman Kunan, to Beirut Pakbara, and to the boy who didn't live to see this anniversary.

130
00:14:23,166 --> 00:14:30,127
Here is the part of this story that has nothing to do with mystery, and everything to do with the truth.

131
00:14:30,127 --> 00:14:37,088
In February twenty twenty-three, Duangphet Phromthep, the team's captain, one of the twelve boys who survived that cave, died in England.

132
00:14:37,088 --> 00:14:38,082
He was seventeen.

133
00:14:39,200 --> 00:14:50,066
The coroner's own words, after a full inquest: he was not known to any mental health service, no one could have foreseen it, and there was no third-party involvement of any kind.

134
00:14:50,066 --> 00:14:53,802
The reason, in the coroner's own language, is simply not known.

135
00:14:54,933 --> 00:14:58,988
Twelve boys and their coach walked into a cave in twenty eighteen.

136
00:14:58,988 --> 00:15:00,339
All thirteen walked out.

137
00:15:00,339 --> 00:15:10,815
What happened to them afterward, the trial, the citizenship, the divers still diving, the boy who didn't get to grow old, was never going to fit inside one clean, simple story.

138
00:15:11,933 --> 00:15:20,210
If a rescue with a happy ending still had this much left unsaid, wait until you see the disaster where nothing about the ending was happy at all.

139
00:15:20,210 --> 00:15:23,166
That full story is live on this channel right now.

140
00:15:24,300 --> 00:15:26,024
Subscribe to mindwired.

